      "weld_bead_lab": "Weld Bead Lab",
      "weld_bead_lab_2": "Weld Bead Lab",
      "weld_gauge": "Weld gauge: ",
      "weld_position": "Weld Position",
      "weld_test_coupons": "Weld test coupons: ",
      "weld_types_used": "Weld types used",
      "weldable_without_preheat_in_most_condi": "Weldable without preheat in most conditions. Common mild steel (A36, A572).",
      "welder_career_stories": "Welder Career Stories",
      "welder_career_stories_2": "Welder Career Stories",
      "welder_performance_qualification_wpq_t": "🎓 Welder Performance Qualification (WPQ) — the \"license\"",
      "welder_qual_prep_practice_quiz": "Welder Qual Prep — Practice Quiz",
      "welder_qualification_prep": "Welder Qualification Prep",
      "welder_qualification_prep_2": "Welder Qualification Prep",
      "welder_qualification_the_formal_proces": "Welder qualification = the formal process of proving you can produce welds that meet a specific code (AWS, ASME, API) on specific materials in specific positions. Without current qualification, you cannot legally weld on most jobs that require code-quality work.",
      "welder_s_defect_catalog": "Welder's Defect Catalog",
      "welder_s_defect_catalog_2": "Welder's Defect Catalog",
      "welder_s_defect_catalog_3": "Welder's Defect Catalog",
      "welder_s_lung": "Welder's lung: ",
      "welder_s_stool_chair_for_any_bench_wor": "• Welder's stool / chair for any bench work that lasts >30 min.",
      "welder_training_pipelines_in_maine": "🎓 Welder training pipelines in Maine",
      "welders_do": "Welders do: ",
      "welders_specifically": "Welders specifically: ",
      "welding_can_hurt_or_kill_you_if_you_sk": "Welding can hurt or kill you if you skip the gear.",
      "welding_career_ladder_typical_progress": "Welding career ladder — typical progression",
      "welding_consumables_electrodes_wires_r": "Welding consumables (electrodes, wires, rods, gases, flux) are the variable cost of every weld. Picking the right consumable for the job is half the welder's skill — wrong electrode + same machine + same hands = bad weld + cracked joint + wasted time + rejected work.",
      "welding_engineering_art_sculpture_desi": "Welding engineering, art/sculpture, design-side",
      "welding_fume_is_a_complex_chemical_mix": "Welding fume is a complex chemical mix. The base composition depends on (1) base metal, (2) electrode/filler, (3) coatings/contamination, (4) shielding gas. NIOSH + ACGIH track Permissible Exposure Limits (PELs) for each constituent.",
      "welding_fume_what_s_in_it_why_it_matte": "☁ Welding fume — what's in it + why it matters",
      "welding_helmet_shade_selection": "Welding helmet shade selection",
      "welding_in_maine": "Welding in Maine",
      "welding_inherently_puts_the_haz_throug": "Welding inherently puts the HAZ through this temperature range. The longer it stays there, the more sensitization.",
      "welding_inspection_cwi": "Welding Inspection (CWI)",
      "welding_inspection_cwi_prep": "Welding Inspection / CWI Prep",
      "welding_involves_significant_voltage_c": "Welding involves significant voltage + current. Most welders never get electrocuted, but the conditions for it exist constantly. Confined-space welding adds atmospheric hazards on top of everything else.",
      "welding_is_more_hazardous_than_most_pe": "Welding is more hazardous than most people realize. The visible risks (arc flash, burns, fire) are real but actually less likely to disable you than the cumulative ones (fume inhalation, hearing loss, joint damage, vibration). PPE & Safety covered the basics. This module goes deeper into what welders actually get hurt by, and what protects you over a 40-year career.",
      "welding_itself_is_moderate_85_95_db_gr": "Welding itself is moderate (85-95 dB); grinding (95-110 dB) + chipping + adjacent processes push past safe limits.",
      "welding_machines_have_60_100v_at_the_e": "Welding machines have 60-100V at the electrode when not arcing. Enough to shock you, especially if you're sweaty or standing on wet ground. Worse with AC than DC.",
      "welding_math_blueprints": "Welding Math + Blueprints",
      "welding_math_blueprints_2": "Welding Math + Blueprints",
      "welding_metal_joining_heat_input_physi": "Welding & metal joining — heat-input physics, bead geometry, defect ID, AWS symbols, OSHA-aligned safety, and Maine welding careers (Bath Iron Works, EMCC, AWS).",
      "welding_metallurgy": "Welding Metallurgy",
      "welding_metallurgy_2": "Welding Metallurgy",
      "welding_metallurgy_is_the_science_of_w": "Welding metallurgy is the science of what happens to metal when you heat it past its melting point, mix in filler, and let it cool. Most welding failures happen here — not in arc striking, not in technique — but in metallurgical changes the welder didn't understand. A skilled welder + bad metallurgy choices = cracked weld. A novice + correct procedure = sound weld.",
      "welding_outside_in_rain_on_damp_concre": "Welding outside in rain, on damp concrete, in tanks with residual moisture = significantly higher shock risk. Use rubber-soled boots, dry gloves, insulating mats.",
      "welding_pays_well_but_can_demand_long_": "Welding pays well but can demand: long shifts, OT, travel for shutdown work. Many welders earn high incomes by working unsustainable hours. Plan early career around saving for shop equipment, education, real estate — assets that produce income beyond your body. The welders who do best long-term have a Plan B beyond their hands.",
      "welding_performance_qualifications": "Welding Performance Qualifications",
      "welding_position": "Welding position",
      "welding_procedure_qualifications": "Welding Procedure Qualifications",
      "welding_procedure_specification_wps_th": "📋 Welding Procedure Specification (WPS) — the \"recipe\"",
      "welding_process": "Welding Process",
      "welding_process_2": "Welding process",
      "welding_process_3": "Welding process",
      "welding_produces_uv_260_400_nm_visible": "Welding produces UV (260-400 nm) + visible (400-700 nm) + IR (700-2500 nm). All three damage eyes. Damage from UV is the most common — arc flash (photokeratitis) feels like sand in your eyes 6-12 hours later. Repeated exposure → cataracts + retinal damage.",
      "welding_rebar_5_for_a_foundation_repai": "Welding rebar (#5) for a foundation repair",
      "welding_safety_quick_reference_card": "Welding-safety quick reference card",
      "welding_shops_shipyards_construction_s": "Welding shops + shipyards + construction sites have distinct cultures. Some are welcoming + supportive; some are rough + exclusionary. Career-long welders need realistic strategies for the workplace itself, not just the welding.",
      "welding_specific_issues": "Welding-specific issues: ",
